# Oracle pin — the COMMITTED, mirror-stable anchor for the conformance gate.
#
# tools/oracle-bootstrap.sh reads this to know what to build. The binaries
# themselves (output/s4-oracles/{validate-peer,entity-peer}) are gitignored, so
# this file is the only committed record of the gate's provenance.
#
#   ref                   — a commit (pre-release) or release tag. When it stops
#                            resolving in the sibling repo, the bootstrap script falls
#                            back to the sibling working-tree HEAD and warns.
#   core_gate_fingerprint — AUTHORITATIVE, mirror-stable identity: the normalized
#                            category set + 53-type floor from profile.go (NOT the raw
#                            file bytes) that define `--profile core`. If a rebuilt
#                            oracle reproduces this fingerprint, its core surface is
#                            identical to what the cohort converged against, no matter
#                            the commit hash and no matter any comment/format churn.
#   core_gate_sha256      — informational only: raw sha256 of the file, moves on any
#                            edit (incl. a comment reword). NOT the anchor.
#
# Re-pin deliberately (policy §4) only when core_gate_fingerprint changes; extension-
# only go bumps and comment rewords move neither the fingerprint nor any verdict.
#
# 2026-07-10 re-normalization: the public entity-core-go mirror rewrote history, so the
# original pinned e8524ed no longer resolves. Re-pinned to the reproducible public HEAD
# cc1970f. Its core gate is PROVABLY the same gate the cohort converged against: the two
# profile.go blobs differ only by a V8 release-prep comment reword (e8524ed raw sha256
# e09a865… -> cc1970f raw sha256 74e04e3…), which leaves the normalized fingerprint
# below untouched. The whole cohort's --profile core 0-FAIL verdict therefore carries
# forward to cc1970f unchanged (CONFORMANCE-MATRIX §3).
#
# 2026-07-27 bucket-B re-pin -> af8a582, AND the reason this file gained a second
# anchor. cc1970f -> af8a582 added four hard-FAIL vectors INSIDE existing core
# categories (connectivity/handshake_nonce_single_use, authz/f40_id_scope_*,
# concurrency/t1_4_frame_write_atomicity) and flipped most of the cohort from PASS to
# FAIL — while core_gate_fingerprint stayed BYTE-IDENTICAL, because no category and no
# floor type moved. Verified by `strings` on both binaries: none of the four names
# exist in the cc1970f build; all four exist in the af8a582 build; fingerprint
# 8261a033… on both.
#
# So the carry-forward rule this file used to state is WRONG as written, and is
# withdrawn:
#
#   core_gate_fingerprint answers "which categories run". It CANNOT answer "what do
#   those categories assert". A matching fingerprint is NOT evidence that a verdict
#   carries forward.
#
#   check_set_digest — the sorted set of declared check names across
#   cmd/internal/validate — is the anchor for carry-forward. Both must match, or the
#   cohort re-runs. tools/oracle-bootstrap.sh now enforces exactly that (its
#   "nothing to do" short-circuit used to compare the fingerprint alone, which would
#   have declared a stale cc1970f oracle current at af8a582 and silently run the OLD
#   check set over all 43 peers).
#
# 2026-07-28 re-pin -> fceb61f, arch's response to the two oracle items keystone asked
# for in HANDOFF-TO-ARCH-2026-07-27-af8a582-cohort-remeasurement.md §7 (F49/F50 are
# still open; this is the F40-attribution + RT-6-class-split half). core_gate_fingerprint
# is BYTE-IDENTICAL to af8a582 (8261a033…, verified — no category or floor type moved);
# check_set_digest MOVED (f3a1516d… -> 3cfd272f…) because one check was added
# (f40_id_scope_include_control) and f40_id_scope_exclude_literal's scoring rewrote
# in place (same declared name, different behaviour) — so per the rule two lines above,
# the cohort re-runs regardless of the unchanged fingerprint.
#
# What moved, precisely (verified against entity-core-go fceb61f, `cmd/internal/validate/{authz,connectivity_f12}.go`):
#   - F40: f40_id_scope_exclude_literal is no longer scored on Row B (the exclude probe)
#     in isolation. It is now the A->B differential against the new control row
#     f40_id_scope_include_control (Row A, include-only, no exclude):
#       (ALLOW,ALLOW) = literal id-scope match, exclude never fired          -> PASS
#       (ALLOW,DENY)  = the exclude entry alone flipped ALLOW->DENY          -> FAIL cause=canonicalization
#       (DENY,DENY)   = baseline itself denies, unrelated to id-scope typing -> WARN, exonerate (NOT an F40 defect)
#       (DENY,ALLOW)  = adding an exclude widened access, incoherent         -> WARN harness/setup error
#     This is the fix for the swift false-positive recorded in the af8a582 handoff §3
#     (a bare 403 could not previously be attributed to canonicalization vs. an
#     unrelated delegation denial; Row A is the control that makes it attributable).
#     Both rows' (status, code, allow) are emitted in CheckResult.Details for keystone's
#     differential tooling to consume directly — see
#     research/diagnostics/f40-scope-typing-differential.py.
#   - RT-6: the replayed-authenticate FAIL is now a labelled ladder, carried in
#     details.rt6_class, so "wrong-but-safe" and "the actual anti-replay hole" never
#     share a verdict:
#       401 invalid_nonce                          -> PASS
#       401, different code                        -> WARN (gate-green, not RT-6-conformant)
#       2xx / accepted                              -> FAIL  rt6_class=replay-accepted   (security)
#       post-delivery close, no response frame      -> FAIL  rt6_class=replay-accepted   (security; scored WITH the
#                                                       accepted-replay class in this build — see the open item below)
#       non-401 rejection (e.g. 409)                -> FAIL  rt6_class=wrong-status       (wrong-but-safe)
#       pre-delivery write-fail close (replay never delivered) -> WARN (unattributable, not scored FAIL)
#     Previously (af8a582 and earlier) BOTH close cases above scored PASS ("baseline
#     holds") — that was an overclaim per [ADR-0012] (a close proves nothing; see the
#     af8a582 handoff §2 class 5). This build corrects it, but has NOT yet split the
#     post-delivery silent-close case into its own class distinct from a true 2xx
#     accept — arch's ruling (2026-07-28, verbal) is that post-delivery silent close
#     should be FAIL rt6_class=no-rejection-proof, NOT replay-accepted, since a close
#     doesn't prove acceptance the way a 2xx does (same non-conflation discipline as
#     the F40 attribution fix). That is a one-line rename pending on entity-core-go's
#     side (tracked there, not here) with zero effect on go/rust/python (the trio all
#     hit true 401 PASS). Keystone's RT-6 reporting must treat rt6_class=replay-accepted
#     on a peer whose underlying behaviour was actually a close (not a 2xx) as
#     provisionally no-rejection-proof until the rename lands — check details.status
#     (0/absent => close; 2xx => true accept) rather than trusting the label alone.
# 2026-08-16 re-pin -> de8f807. Direct instruction was to hold at fceb61f until arch's
# in-flight vector work stabilized; arch's Q23-install / peers-dimension sign-off (relayed
# 2026-08-16) cleared that hold. core_gate_fingerprint is BYTE-IDENTICAL to fceb61f
# (8261a033…, verified — same 16-category core set, no category or floor type moved);
# check_set_digest MOVED (3cfd272f… -> 43c23708…) so per policy the cohort re-runs
# regardless of the unchanged fingerprint.
#
# Prerequisite fixed first, per W1: tools/oracle-bootstrap.sh's check_set_digest() grepped
# only `\.Declare\(`, blind to the `.DeclareSelf(` method arch added for offline/
# client-free checks (35 sites at de8f807) — a naive re-pin would have under-counted the
# check set from day one. Regex is now `\.Declare(Self)?\(`; verified against de8f807's
# tree: 1183 `.Declare(` + 35 `.DeclareSelf(` = 1139 unique names (old regex would have
# seen 1104 and silently dropped 35).
#
# What moved, precisely (fceb61f..de8f807 is 309 commits, not the 233 originally recorded
# here -- corrected 2026-08-17 after arch flagged the discrepancy in their sign-off
# (SIGNOFF-2026-08-16-vector-set-final.md §2: they measured 305 against a slightly earlier
# HEAD); re-verified directly against entity-core-go with both `git log --oneline
# fceb61f..de8f807` and `--first-parent`, both give 309. Likely a public-mirror history
# rewrite artifact per arch's own note. The check-set digest (not the commit count) is what
# gated the re-pin decision, so this correction changes no verdict -- diffed by check name
# AND by `git log -- <core-category files>` to catch behavioral changes under an unchanged
# name, not just new/removed names):
#
#   - check-name diff: 82 added, 4 renamed (content_get_strict66_* -> content_get_hex_
#     strictness_*, serving_mode — not core). Of the 82 additions, the overwhelming
#     majority are pure-extension (signaling, registry_issuer, encryption, discovery,
#     local_files, published_root, serving_mode, substitute, revision, liveness,
#     reachability, compute — none in the 16-category `--profile core` set). Verified via
#     `inCoreProfile()` / suite.go:757: a category not in coreProfileCategories is SKIPPED
#     WHOLESALE under `--profile core`, not run-and-scored — so none of those checks touch
#     our cohort's gate.
#
#   - **CAUTION, corrects the framing in arch's 2026-08-16 sign-off message**: Q23
#     install-time (`q23_install_builtin_apply_capability_resource`,
#     `q23_install_builtin_apply_resource_only`) lives in `cmd/internal/validate/compute.go`
#     under `catCompute = "compute"`, which is NOT one of the 16 `coreProfileCategories`.
#     It is a real, correctly-landed MUST, but it does not gate `--profile core` and our
#     `run-cohort-census.sh` runs `-profile core` exclusively (never `-profile full`) — so
#     these two checks will report SKIP "outside --profile core" for every peer in this
#     census, not PASS/FAIL. The three-way rust/py lock they're waiting on is real but
#     orthogonal to this re-pin.
#
#   - **Five checks are genuinely new AND inside core categories** (the actual gating
#     surface of this re-pin):
#       - `authz_peers_target_from_uri` (authz) — B1: live coverage of the §5.2 `peers`
#         dimension, paired with go's own B2 fix (FindMatchingGrant tested local_peer_id
#         instead of extract_peer(execute.uri), and skipped the check when `peers` was
#         absent) — this is the exact oracle-host bug + coverage gap keystone flagged in
#         HANDOFF-TO-ARCH-2026-08-13-peers-grant-dimension-oracle-gap.md; both items close
#         with this pin.
#       - `below_threshold_denied_write` (multisig) — go commit 2e8e6d0 (G-11): the whole
#         multisig category was read-only (Operation "get" hardcoded in every check's
#         request-builder), so a peer verifying joint authority on read but not write scored
#         11/11 and still accepted a forged joint authority to write. New check drives a
#         `put`. Same commit ALSO found `below_threshold_rejected` (pre-existing, name
#         unchanged) was vacuously testing the M6 root-trust rule instead of M4 threshold —
#         it now SKIPs against an ephemeral peer where it used to false-PASS; the census runs
#         NAMED peers (per this repo's `--name` convention), so it stays PASS there, but note
#         the mechanism if an ephemeral-peer run ever shows a new multisig SKIP.
#       - `expired_capability_denied_write` (security) — go commit 08c1cf2 (G-1a): the whole
#         temporal-expiry deny family (`expired_capability_denied` et al.) only ever drove a
#         `system/tree` GET; new check drives a `put`, closing the same read-only-coverage
#         shape as multisig above.
#       - `core_register_reserved_refused` + `core_register_reserved_publishes_nothing`
#         (handlers) — §6.6 register-at-reserved-pattern refusal, plus the negative half (did
#         a 403 leak state anyway). No negative half existed before 2026-08-11.
#     All five verified present in the built binary (`strings output/s4-oracles/validate-peer`).
#
#   - Scanned every commit fceb61f..de8f807 touching a core-category file for behavior
#     changes under an unchanged check name (not just the name-diff, per W1's own warning
#     that a name-level diff is necessary but not sufficient): `type_system`
#     (commit 72001f5, a WARN-severity type-descriptor constraint fix — severity unchanged,
#     message content only) and the `08c1cf2` deny-family (comment/rationale annotations
#     only, explicitly no functional change) were the only other core-category touches;
#     neither flips a PASS/FAIL verdict. `crypto_agility` (G-12, 0f78a84) reclassified 4
#     checks `Declare`->`DeclareSelf` (offline KATs, never touched the peer) — attribution-
#     only, no peer's score changes. Everything else touching validate/*.go in this range
#     (reachability, signaling, liveness, registry_issuer, registry — the R-1/R-2/R-5/R-6
#     items) is in extension-only categories, outside `--profile core`.
#
# Full detail: go's own session report `docs/validation/reports/2026-08-16-f-peers-
# dimension-fix-and-q23-install-time-gate.md` (entity-core-go, read live at de8f807).
